 {
  "key": "integ.by-parts.q13",
  "type": "EXTENDED_RESPONSE",
  "concepts": [
   "amaths.integ.by-parts"
  ],
  "difficulty": 5,
  "marks": 4,
  "estimatedSeconds": 360,
  "stem": "Let $I = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\mathrm{e}^{x}\\sin x\\,dx$. By integrating by parts twice, show that $I = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{e}^{\\pi} + 1}{2}$.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "M1 first application: $I = \\Big[-\\mathrm{e}^{x}\\cos x\\Big]_{0}^{\\pi} + \\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\mathrm{e}^{x}\\cos x\\,dx = \\mathrm{e}^{\\pi} + 1 + \\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\mathrm{e}^{x}\\cos x\\,dx$",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "M1 second application: $\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\mathrm{e}^{x}\\cos x\\,dx = \\Big[\\mathrm{e}^{x}\\sin x\\Big]_{0}^{\\pi} - I = -I$",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p3",
      "description": "M1 forms the equation $I = \\mathrm{e}^{\\pi} + 1 - I$",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p4",
      "description": "A1 solves to $I = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{e}^{\\pi} + 1}{2}$",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "modelAnswer": "With $u = \\mathrm{e}^{x}$ both times (or $u = \\sin x$, then $u = \\cos x$, consistently):\n\n$$\nI = \\Big[-\\mathrm{e}^{x}\\cos x\\Big]_{0}^{\\pi} + \\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\mathrm{e}^{x}\\cos x\\,dx = (\\mathrm{e}^{\\pi} + 1) + \\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\mathrm{e}^{x}\\cos x\\,dx\n$$\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\mathrm{e}^{x}\\cos x\\,dx = \\Big[\\mathrm{e}^{x}\\sin x\\Big]_{0}^{\\pi} - \\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\mathrm{e}^{x}\\sin x\\,dx = 0 - I\n$$\n\n$$\nI = \\mathrm{e}^{\\pi} + 1 - I \\Rightarrow I = \\frac{\\mathrm{e}^{\\pi} + 1}{2}\n$$"
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "The original integral reappears, so treat I as an unknown and solve for it.",
  "hints": [
   "Keep the same type of choice for u in both applications.",
   "cos π = −1 and sin π = 0."
  ],
  "skill": "ANALYSIS",
  "calculator": false
 },
